WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.622 --> 00:00:01.982
(dramatic instrumental music)

00:00:01.982 --> 00:00:02.815
(buzzer blares)

00:00:02.815 --> 00:00:04.380
- [TV Announcer] Never hug or kiss them.

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:07.230
Never let them sit in your lap.

00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:10.620
Mother love is a dangerous instrument.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.810
- [Narrator] Today,
you're not likely to find

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:14.150
a popular parenting book

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.403
that warns you about
cuddling your own baby.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.030
But take a look into history,

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.530
and you'll see a time when
touch was deemed taboo.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:25.170
And one psychologist
was setting out to prove

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.653
that love isn't such a bad thing.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:30.860
(man groaning)

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:33.430
- The early 20th century psychologists

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.300
were all about trying to make science

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.250
kind of a thing of
formulas and calculations.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:44.570
Relationships, and even worse
affectionate relationships,

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.290
become something that just doesn't enter

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.490
into the calculations.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.710
- [Narrator] This is Deborah Blum,

00:00:49.710 --> 00:00:52.580
science journalist and
author of "Love at Goon Park:

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.500
Harry Harlow and the
Science of Affection".

00:00:55.500 --> 00:00:59.360
- This is a white male-dominated science.

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.620
These are men who are often
not in the least involved

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.050
in parenting, themselves.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:07.490
And in the white male version of reality,

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.100
everyone had their place,

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:11.350
and women's place was in the home,

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:14.390
and children were to be seen, not heard.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:18.830
Love and affection were
messy, girly things.

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.150
- [Narrator] And to be taken seriously,

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:24.780
no scientists had time for
such messy, girly things.

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:28.600
Instead, people like Freud,
Pavlov, Skinner and others

00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.080
converged to explain the mysteries

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:34.060
of the human mind and body
in far less sentimental terms

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:37.540
- You saw a push to
establish human behavior

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.760
as sort of a series of
drives and responses.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.980
The only important part
of the relationship

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.170
between mother and child

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.210
is that the mother
satisfies the hunger drive

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:50.880
and feeds the baby.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:52.800
- [Narrator] Plus, any signs that a child

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.000
returned their mother's affection

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.583
were simply brushed off
as classical conditioning.

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:02.480
And one person making this
point was John Watson.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.450
- One of the things that John
Watson found really important

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.580
was what you might think
of as conditioning a baby.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.136
We know this from Pavlov's dog experiment.

00:02:12.136 --> 00:02:13.740
(bell jingling)
(dog panting)

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.440
Watson believed you could
do this with babies as well.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.320
- [Narrator] In fact, he said mothers

00:02:19.320 --> 00:02:22.771
over-condition their
babies by coddling them.

00:02:22.771 --> 00:02:24.900
(dramatic orchestral music)

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:27.290
- Mothers should not
pick up their children.

00:02:27.290 --> 00:02:29.750
If you held them too much
or comforted them too much,

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:31.853
you would destroy their moral fiber.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.040
- [Narrator] Watson's book
even goes as far as to say

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.920
that mother love is a
dangerous instrument.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:38.960
- Wait a minute.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.320
- [Narrator] And may wreck your adult son

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:42.560
or daughter's vocational future.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:44.580
- Show me why I shouldn't fire you!

00:02:44.580 --> 00:02:47.760
- [Narrator] And their
chances for marital happiness.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.772
- And do you wanna do that to your child?

00:02:49.772 --> 00:02:50.880
(baby crying)

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:52.420
Of course you don't, right?

00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:55.970
So people actually set
aside their own instincts.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:57.210
- [Narrator] There were a few people

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.500
who disagreed with Watson's advice.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.530
- There were doctors
and also psychologists

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:04.670
who started to see health issues

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:08.060
related to this kind of
isolation of touch and affection.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.760
- Stressing experiences in childhood

00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:12.523
will produce mental conflict.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.700
- [Narrator] But unfortunately,
many people speaking out...

00:03:16.700 --> 00:03:18.720
- [Man] You nincompoop!

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:20.290
- [Narrator] Were mocked for not having

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:21.501
enough scientific proof.

00:03:21.501 --> 00:03:22.380
(laughing)

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:24.150
It would take a number of years

00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:26.920
for someone to emerge with
the right amount of gumption

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:28.765
to challenge these ideas head on.

00:03:28.765 --> 00:03:30.500
(cheering)

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.770
That person was Harry Harlow.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.700
- He was a poetry writing, chain smoking,

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:38.260
philandering, alcoholic, psychologist,

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.430
completely work-obsessed,

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.403
and he was a complete street fighter.

00:03:44.080 --> 00:03:46.130
I'm going to say this,
and you can bleep me out.

00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.060
He would just take notes
no (monkey screeches)

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.300
when he had an idea that
he thought was worth doing.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.230
- [Narrator] Harlow started
up a primate laboratory

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:56.320
in the 1930s to study monkey intelligence.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.470
But as time went on,

00:03:57.470 --> 00:04:00.110
he and his students
noticed something very odd.

00:04:00.110 --> 00:04:02.670
- Mothers who would reject their babies,

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:05.030
those babies became very attached

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.780
to the blankets that they put in the cage,

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:10.790
and when they had to remove
the babies from the cage,

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:14.140
the babies were traumatized
by letting the blanket go.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:16.600
- [Narrator] Which made them
wonder, what's going on?

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.900
- He was married at that
time to a child psychologist,

00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:23.740
Margaret Kuenne, who saw the
parallels with human children,

00:04:23.740 --> 00:04:25.900
and so they decided to just look at touch.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:29.953
Does the baby get something
out of being able to cuddle?

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:32.730
- [Narrator] They came up with a plan

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.000
to raise newborn monkeys in isolation

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.900
and gave them fake surrogate mothers.

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:41.720
- [Harlow] These are the only
mothers these babies ever had.

00:04:41.720 --> 00:04:45.890
- One of them was a cloth
mom, and she was very cuddly,

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.410
and one was a wire mom
who wasn't cuddly at all,

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.890
she was made of metal.

00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:53.960
They would put both the
moms in the same cage.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.540
The cloth mom was just there,

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:58.743
but the wire mom had the
good stuff, the milk.

00:04:58.743 --> 00:05:00.020
- [Harlow] Watch.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:02.180
- [Deborah] And what they found
was every single one of them

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:03.820
would go over a wire mom to feed.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:05.270
- [Man] He's got to eat to live.

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.030
- [Deborah] Then go back to cloth mom.

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:08.370
- [Narrator] That alone went against

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:10.020
all of those early theories.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.940
- [Deborah] The only important part

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.332
is that the mother feeds the baby.

00:05:14.332 --> 00:05:15.810
(buzzer blares)

00:05:15.810 --> 00:05:18.177
- [Narrator] Here's where
things start to get scarier.

00:05:18.177 --> 00:05:20.173
(suspenseful orchestral music)

00:05:20.173 --> 00:05:21.713
- [Man] It looks diabolical.

00:05:21.713 --> 00:05:23.830
- They would put them in
a strange situation room

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:25.250
with a weird toy,

00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.680
and they would see how the
baby monkey would respond.

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.380
- [Narrator] And the difference
in reactions was huge.

00:05:32.507 --> 00:05:33.340
(monkey screeches)

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:34.660
- [Man] He's scared, all right.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.160
- With cloth mom in the room,

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:38.960
these babies would run
to their mom, hold on,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.030
and then they'd get really brave.

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:45.160
They'd go challenge the
evil monster and yell at it.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:46.890
But if they had been raised by wire mom,

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:48.510
it didn't even matter
if she was in the room;

00:05:48.510 --> 00:05:49.740
they just collapsed.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:53.220
And they started realizing
that touch is comfort

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.560
and that comfort is confidence,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.863
the exact opposite of
what John Watson said.

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.470
- [Narrator] In 1958,
Harlow shared his findings

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:07.340
in a speech called The Nature of Love.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:08.880
- And then he doesn't mess around.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.280
He says, this is love.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.603
This is not some weird
drive response thing.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:18.620
These babies love their
mothers, and they need that.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:20.658
- And you can imagine the response, right?

00:06:20.658 --> 00:06:22.949
(audience cheering)

00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:25.430
Well, not exactly.

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.279
- The psychology community was horrified.

00:06:28.279 --> 00:06:29.112
(woman shrieks)

00:06:29.112 --> 00:06:32.079
What have you done? You
used the word "love".

00:06:32.079 --> 00:06:32.912
(bangs table)

00:06:32.912 --> 00:06:34.490
You know that's not in a single textbook.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:35.600
- [Narrator] Okay, so some of them

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.840
still weren't quite ready to hear it,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.020
but they would come around eventually.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.580
- Publicly the response was much more

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:46.130
people recognizing that this was real.

00:06:46.130 --> 00:06:48.640
Science is trying to cling desperately

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:50.170
that love is meaningless,

00:06:50.170 --> 00:06:52.770
and the rest of us are
starting to say you're wrong.

00:06:54.097 --> 00:06:55.250
- [Narrator] Harlow's experiments

00:06:55.250 --> 00:06:58.140
helped inspire a big paradigm shift,

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:00.910
but it would be remiss
to ignore the backlash

00:07:00.910 --> 00:07:02.510
that also followed.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.990
- We see ourselves in these experiments.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:08.470
You cannot look at a baby
monkey and not see a human.

00:07:08.470 --> 00:07:11.970
And I think it's why the
work is so condemned.

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:13.860
- [Narrator] There's no
doubt that Harlow's work

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.860
traumatized monkeys, and with
that came public outrage.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.970
- He didn't mean to, but
he forced the setting

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:24.870
of some ethical standards and lines

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:27.470
that people just said we
can't cross these lines.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:30.520
It made him a poster child for
the animal rights movement,

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.523
and you can't argue that there's
not some reason for that.

00:07:36.932 --> 00:07:39.120
(bright electronic music)

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:40.820
- Harry Harlow wasn't afraid to say

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.283
that love and touch matter.

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.540
And by fighting for
science to recognize that,

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:49.810
he also changed the futures
of kids growing up today.

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:53.530
- And I say that growing up
in the post-Harlow generation,

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:57.810
love is an essential part of who we are.

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:01.500
It defines many of our most
important relationships.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:04.980
That's what a secure attachment is:

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.210
the fearlessness to face the world,

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.003
because if you do fall,
someone has your back.

